From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Fri Jan  8 07:19:43 1999
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian D Ripley)
Date: Fri, 08 Jan 1999 07:19:43 +0000
Subject: Re: [S] Q: missing values and tree()
In-Reply-To: <199901080533.XAA09158@mars.stat.wisc.edu>
References: <199901080533.XAA09158@mars.stat.wisc.edu>
Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.05.9901080710430.4317-100000@auk.stats>

On Thu, 7 Jan 1999, Tjen-Sien Lim wrote:

> 
> I'm wondering how S-Plus actually handles missing numerical covariate
> values in tree() function when I specify
> "na.action=na.tree.replace.all". The tree() function will discretize
> the numerical covariates containing missing values into several
> intervals. I'd like to know how tree() constructs those
> intervals. Thanks in advance for any pointers.

First, that function is risky, and is really not recommended. From the
help page for na.tree.replace:

WARNING:
       The use of na.tree.replace.all  for  continuous  variables
       with  missing  values  might  be  risky.  These continuous
       predictors  are  replaced  by  factors  based   on   their
       quartiles.  This  migh  be  a good exploratory step but it

which I think also answers your question. It cuts on the quartiles,
sets the NAs to "NA" (as a string) and makes a factor.

If you need to do this, both rpart (at statlib, surrogate splits) and my
Tree function (http://www.stats.ox.ac.uk/pub/S or SWin, case splitting)
have much better strategies for missing values based on imputation.
Treating NA as a separate level is only a good idea when NA is informative
(i.e. didn't do the test because it was bound to be normal) AND you have
lots of examples in the training set.

