> Any ideas gratefully received!!

        I had a similar problem a couple of days ago, and found a way to
do it. I wouldn't call it a solution because the shell program really
doesn't look very nice...

        It's written for tsch and goes like this:

#!/local/bin/tcsh -f

Splus BATCH batch.d.in batch.d.out
sleep 10
grep 'end-of-file' batch.d.out > pid
ls -s pid > str
set s=`awk '{print $1}' str`
while($s<1)
  sleep 30
  grep 'end-of-file' batch.d.out > pid
  ls -s pid > str
  set s=`awk '{print $1}' str`
end

        As one can see: The idea is to keep the shell-program waiting
until it finds the line 'end-of-file' in the batch-out-file which means
that all of the commands in the batch-in-file is finished. The way I wrote
my batch-file anyway...

        I would call this a piece of dirty shell-programming, and even if
it works I'm looking forward to see a better solution from someone with a
bit more shell-programming experience than me ;-)

