I'm using Splus 3.4 under Unix/Solaris/SunOS 5.5.1.

Essentially what I'm trying to do is pass the name of a file
containing the compiled code of a Fortran subroutine to an Splus
function, so that different things happen depending on what name is
given.  The key issue is that this subroutine is called by
***another*** subroutine which is the one invoked by .Fortran()
within my Splus function.  (Sorry if that seem convoluted, but it
does make sense.)  This seems workable with dyn.load() and I thought
from the documentation that it would work with dyn.load.shared also.

Here's a toy example.

My Splus function looks like:

foo <- function(sub,n) {
        dyn.load.shared('./mylib.so')
        dyn.load.shared(paste('./',sub,'.so',sep=''),symbols=symbol.For('xxx'))
        .Fortran(
                "bigsub",
                n=as.integer(n),
                a=double(1)
        )$a
}

I have Fortran/ratfor files

bigsub.r:

        subroutine bigsub(n,a)
        double precision a
        call xxx(n,a)
        return
        end

dummy.r:
        subroutine xxx(n,a)
        double precision a
        a = 0.d0
        return
        end

smart1.r:
        subroutine xxx(n,a)
        double precision a
        a = n
        return
        end

and smart2.r:
        subroutine xxx(n,a)
        double precision a
        a = 2*n
        return
        end

I create the file mylib.so by

        f77 -c bigsub.r dummy.r
        Splus SHLIB -o mylib.so bigsub.o dummy.o

and the file smart1.so by

        f77 -c smart1.r
        Splus SHLIB -o smart1.so smart1.o

(and similarly for smart2.so.)

I then start Splus, execute

        > foo('smart1',42)

and get

        [1] 0 

and

        > foo('smart2',42)

and get

        [1] 0 

again, and not 42 and 84 as I had wished.  I ***thought*** from
reading the documentation that specifying "symbols = symbol.For("xxx"))"
in the second invocation of dyn.load.shared would "force in" the new
definition of ``xxx'' (coming from the ``smart1'' or ``smart2'' code)
overriding the old definition of ``xxx'' coming from the ``dummy''
code orginally loaded into mylib.so.  Apparently not.

Is dyn.load.shared not working as it is claimed to work, or am I
misunderstanding something?  More to the point I guess, can anyone
suggest a way of doing what I want to do, using dyn.load.shared?

